GS CPU rasterizer: the same pixels with less work per pixel. A pixel's address uses the first block's page table (every block's table is that one plus the block's offset, so 4 to 32 KB stay in the cache instead of 32 times that), and the address, read and write functions are forced inline with the bit widths folded. The VRAM reads and writes of the backend go through inline switches on the format instead of function tables, and texture reads inline GSMem::ReadTexture. WritePixel's state (scissor, fog, frame and depth buffers, tests, blend selectors) and SampleTexture's (wrap modes, formats, CLUT, page cache) are worked out once per triangle or sprite. Triangles step their integer edge functions from pixel to pixel and scan each row only over the run the edges allow (widened by a pixel each way, every pixel still tested). Bilinear filtering does its four channels in one SSE pass with the same float operations and the same rounding (half away from zero, without lroundf). A CLUT load identical to the last one in its context (the main path or a band), from source bytes that are still the same in the texture page cache, is skipped, since it would write what the table holds; the cache is moved exactly as the full load would move it. Checked in Metropolismania: all 4 MB of VRAM is byte-identical at the eleven views of a scripted city camera run with frame skip off (two runs before the change also match each other), PS2X_GS_BANDS_VERIFY=1 over 15,200 banded runs found no difference, and PS2Recomp's tests give the same results. Explore autoplay, frame skip off: 28.2 to 33.4 fps, the band threads from about 60% to 40% busy, CLUT loading from 11% of their time to under 1%.

